History Channel's hit reality television  show Pawn Stars has been on the air for  ten years and it's certainly been an  eventful decade the show has had its  fair share of heartfelt moments and  tense situations here are the most  unforgettable moments in Pawn Stars  history in one of the most iconic  moments from the first season Rick big  Hoss and Chumlee all worked together to  pull a fast one on the old man they  arranged to have his beloved 1966  Chrysler Imperial towed from the gold  and Silver's back lot to be given a full  restoration there's a twist they do so  in secret so when the old man comes into  the store asking where his car is Rick  tells his father that they finally sold  the thing hey Corey what Rick where's my  damn Imperial sold it you what I thought  it was just gonna sit there and rot so  we sold it for a thousand bucks after  the old man threatens to unleash the  wrath of God Chumlee goes even further  informing the old man that this  fictional buyer was planning on turning  the rare classic into a lowrider with  hydraulics and a cheetah skin interior  the old man storms off after cursing at  his son and grandson a few weeks later  the car's finished and Rick brings the  old man to the back lot to reveal the  gift for his 50th wedding anniversary  the old man loves his newly restored car  and even calls it a work of art watching  the old man go from furious to ecstatic  makes this one of the best moments of  the show's entire run in the sixth  season episode say it ain't so a  customer comes into the store with a  book signed by baseball player Shoeless  Joe Jackson not only was Jackson a  legendary baseball player he was  illiterate so his signature is  incredibly rare naturally Rick asks for  any documentation that may support the  customers story to his surprise the  customer produces a letter of  authentication and Rick decides to buy  the book on the spot for $13,000 before  the customer can change his mind and  sell it somewhere else  afterwards Rick brings in an  Authenticator to see if the signature is  legitimate since he hadn't heard of the  Authenticator who signed the customer's  letter  unfortunately the expert reports that  the signature is likely fake Rick  decides to send the book off to yet  another Authenticator but is given a  laundry list of reasons to be confident  that the signature is a fake all in all  Rick learns about 13 thousand reasons to  double-check that a rare object  is actually rare during the season 1  episode Secret Santa Chumlee enters the  pawn shop in a jolly mood with a Santa  outfit to match complementing his outfit  is a bushy white beard in a sack full of  presents Cory quickly tells Chumlee that  the role of Santa has already been taken  for the day murder Christmas tree won't  Chumbley comes back to work later  dressed as an elf eliciting laughs from  the elder Harrisons at the end of the  day the cast of Pawn Stars all exchange  gifts as part of a Secret Santa  arrangement everyone's gifts are small  except for Rick's present - Chumlee when  he first lays eyes on the watch Rick  Adam Chumlee seems completely shocked  the gesture from Rick demonstrates how  close the group is and how they really  do care for each other  in the first season episode time  machines a man brings in a flintlock  pistol he had bought at a gun show  seeing the potential for an interesting  piece of history at his store Corey  calls in an expert in vintage firearms  to appraise the value of the gun the  expert relays that if the gun is  authentic it could fetch a price upwards  of $2,500 unfortunately however the gun  is just a modern recast of an old design  that's only worth about $75 outside the  store the customer says my wife was  pissed when I bought this gun now she's  really gonna kill me  [Music]  being tricked into buying something fake  for a grossly inflated price is a  horrible situation to find oneself in  finding out on national television is  even worse in celebration of Halloween  during the season 4 episode Rick or  Treat the employees at the gold and  silver decide to compete in a costume  contest with a prize of $100 for the  best costume the results aren't exactly  inspiring Corey does the bare minimum  putting on a novelty headband that makes  it look like someone stabbed through his  skull with a knife no one can guess that  rick has dressed up as dr. evil from the  Austin Powers films Chumlee is dressed  up as the then teen heartthrob Justin  Bieber while the old man is dressed up  as rooster cogburn from True Grit  the bickering all ends when Antwon the  security guard walks up to the counter  dressed as a giant baby binky and all  this immediately Nets him the $100 with  the snickering approval of the Harrisons  and Chumlee the season 5 episode Corey's  big play features a customer selling a  set of artifacts from an Arizona prison  carrying a couple of balls and chains  from Yuma prison in an antique Wells  Fargo strongbox the customer hopes to  leave the store with $2,000 upon closer  inspection the balls have the prison  name embossed on them and the chains  appeared to have been electrically  welded making both sets of items in  authentic recreations  however the strongbox passes Rick's  inspections so he begins negotiations at  $400 eventually settling at 450 dollars  Rick secures the Wells Fargo box to  determine the box's value Rick brings in  a museum administrator to appraise the  box but when it is revealed that the box  is a fake the old man chides Rick saying  he suspected the box was fake the entire  time why'd he let Rick make such a big  mistake I didn't want to bust your  bubble  what so you relevee spend the money  instead now I can holler at you after  spending almost a decade on television  Richard old man Harrison passed away in  June 2018 at the age of 77 as a tribute  to his memory the final episode of  season 15 was made into an hour-long  special showcasing the best of the old  man in the special Rick reminisce about  his dad between clips showing just how  much the old man cared of  his family his business and his passions  the episode is nostalgic and intimate  allowing the audience to feel included  in the lives of these people they have  spent the last nine years watching the  highlighted clips of the old man show  him at his best sometimes when he was  happiest other times when he would get  angry with the other employees at the  gold and silver but every scene showed  off his no-nonsense approach to dealing  with others no matter who he was dealing  with the old man would never mince words  and would always play fair making him  one of the most beloved cast members on  the show when a seller named Phil visits  the shop looking to sell his 32 Lincoln  roadster in the season four episode by  the book it isn't exactly hard to get  Rick and the old man to jump out from  behind the pawn shop counter bargaining  and settling on an agreeable price is  another story however Phil wants  $100,000 a price Rick is skeptical about  considering the vehicle isn't in prime  condition according to Rick a 32 Lincoln  roadster can be sold for about one  hundred and seventy thousand dollars in  mint condition but Phil's car has some  wear and tear that diminishes the value  at first it seems like Phil will refuse  to budge on anything lower than $100,000  but then he tells Rick he's willing to  sell the car for $95,000 in gold while  legally Rick can't exchange the old car  for gold Rick can give Phil $95,000 and  then he can then turn around and buy  $95,000 worth the gold from the shop the  32 Lincoln roadster isn't just one of  Rick's most expensive purchases it's one  of the most unique ways anyone on the  show has named their price  I think I'll bury the gold somewhere  where nobody can get it  in the season four episode appropriately  titled face the music Chumlee strikes a  bargain for $1500 with a customer over  what appears to be an authentic Gibson  mandolin  however the bargain was struck before an  expert could be brought in to assess the  instrument big mistake once the expert  is brought in to evaluate the mandolin  it's not only revealed to be a fake but  an obvious one at that in the words of  the expert himself yeah this is fake as  hell man this is I just paid $1,500 for  that now Shh so what exactly gave the  replica way for starters the decal was  cut out with scissors and the G and  Gibson doesn't match the official  branding to make matters worse the  instruments finish appears to be plastic  whereas a genuine Gibson would use  lacquer this long list of issues along  with a few other giveaways makes it a  gut punch for poor Chumlee when he has  to tell Rick and the old man that he  blew $1500 on a bad knockoff so how  exactly is Chumlee supposed to get back  that money in a fitting punishment he's  forced to play the fake mandolin outside  the shop for tips to earn back the loss  of his investment hopefully Chumlee can  play the mandolin as well as that seller  played him in the season 2 episode hell  week corey and chumlee struggled to move  a heavy statue across the store as the  two huff and puff the old man and Rick  take notice and give them grief when  their physical abilities are called into  question Corey asserts that he could  beat Chumlee in any sort of competition  Rick challenges Corey and Chumlee to  compete in an obstacle course with the  stipulation that the loser will have to  buy lunch for a week  the obstacle course consists of a tire  stepping station a jumping jack station  and a jump rope station in the end Corey  ends up winning but after the duo's  clumsy performance neither manages to  impress Rick or the old man watching  these two turkeys run this course is  going to be like watching a grizzly bear  ice skate it ain't gonna be pretty  still watching Corey and Chumlee get  competitive with each other is a rare  sight that makes this episode one of the  most enjoyable in the second season  episode flight of the Chum a customer  visits the gold and silver with a statue  of Perseus and Pegasus made by Emil  Picoult  Rick is familiar with Buchholz work and  he knows that his statues are highly  collectible and worth  thousands of dollars so he gives the  customers item a thorough look  however Rick immediately become  suspicious when he finds markings on the  statue that do not match the period it  was supposedly produced in he then finds  markings engraved on the statue showing  that it was made in the United States  since the artist is French this confirms  that the statue is a reproduction at the  suggestion that the statue may not be  entirely authentic the customer gets  angry with Rick I don't care what you  say I don't care what you tell me but I  know you fool as the tension mounts the  pawnshop security guard Antoine begins  walking toward the customer in case an  intervention is needed however he is  waved off by Rick in the end the old man  diffuses the situation by firmly  explaining that they are not interested  in buying the statue which brings the  memorable interaction to a close check  out one of our newest videos right here  plus even more looper videos about your  favorite shows are coming soon subscribe  to our YouTube channel and hit the bell  so you don't miss a single one  you

